Approach: The classic approach to semilinear parabolic local existence: on X = C_b(ℝ^n) or L^∞, define mild solution of u_t = Δu + f(u), u(0) = u0, via Duhamel: u(t) = e^{tΔ}u0 + ∫₀ᵗ e^{(t-s)Δ} f(u(s)) ds. Use Banach fixed point on C([0,T], X) with the heat semigroup estimates: ‖e^{tΔ}u0‖ ≤ ‖u0‖ and ‖e^{(t-s)Δ} f(u(s))‖ ≤ ‖f(u(s))‖ — L^∞ contraction. Then the map Φ(u)(t) = e^{tΔ}u0 + ∫₀ᵗ e^{(t-s)Δ} f(u(s)) ds is a contraction for T small enough if f is Lipschitz (in L^∞). That gives genuine semilinear parabolic local existence in L^∞/C_b.

The heat semigroup L^∞ estimates needed:
- ‖e^{tΔ}φ‖_{L^∞} ≤ ‖φ‖_{L^∞} (sup of convolution with nonnegative kernel of mass 1)
- e^{tΔ}φ ∈ C_b (continuity in x) — maybe not needed for L^∞ space
- strong continuity at t=0 for nice φ.

